# Phase 3G: original handheld runtime in a private browser

This build executes the production C++ game loop on the server and presents its
240×240 RGB565 display in the browser. Character creation, town, real room
generation, tile movement/collision, menus, door transitions, monster AI,
initiative and combat use the existing production sources. The browser contains
input handling, bounded pixel decoding and scaling; it determines no gameplay
results.

The first playable version supports **one controlling player per server process**.
It does not claim completed authoritative multiplayer gameplay. The existing
GameSession still owns authenticated participant capabilities and reconnect
lifecycle. The original handheld runtime owns the actual character, dungeon and
combat state, composed alongside GameSession by Service. Topology-only movement,
map and event commands are disabled once a playable runtime is claimed, avoiding
two competing world representations. Other participants cannot claim this runtime,
receive its screen or submit its inputs.

## Start the verified NUC build

In Windows PowerShell, open the existing authorized SSH connection:

```powershell
ssh jimbo@nuc-server
```

In that NUC terminal:

```sh
cd /home/jimbo/pathfinder-playable-phase3g.StnP3cas
python3 source/tools/run_private_game.py --server build/apps/server/pfme_server --port 18765 --seed 2
```

The foreground launcher creates a fresh strong development secret, prints it for
you to copy and starts the server with that secret in its child environment.
It writes no credential file, installs no service and does not change shell or
machine settings. Keep this terminal open. Avoid recording or sharing its secret.

Open a **second Windows PowerShell terminal**, then run:

```powershell
ssh -N -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes -o BatchMode=yes -o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes -L 127.0.0.1:18765:127.0.0.1:18765 jimbo@nuc-server
```

Keep that tunnel terminal open. Browse to:

```text
http://127.0.0.1:18765/play
```

Paste the generated secret into Development secret, choose a name without spaces
and click Connect. The password field clears after use. The gate and reconnect
proof remain only in that page’s memory. Reconnect from the same tab; reloading or
closing it loses that proof. The protocol cannot recover ownership from a name or
numeric player ID.

Use the same port at both ends of the tunnel, because Host and Origin are checked
against the listener port. If 18765 is occupied, choose another unused port above
1023 consistently in the launcher, both tunnel port positions and browser URL.
Do not terminate an unrelated process to free a port.

## Keyboard and mouse

| Browser input | Original handheld input |
| --- | --- |
| Down / S | Encoder clockwise |
| Up / W | Encoder counterclockwise |
| Enter | Encoder press |
| Space | Button A |
| Escape | Button B |
| Hold Enter | Original encoder long press (750 ms) |

Onscreen controls emulate the same inputs; they do not select game menu rows
directly. Rotation changes class, weapon style, menu selection or facing according
to the original state. In a dungeon, rotate to face a tile and press Enter to step.
Menu selection usually uses Space or Enter; attack targeting confirms with Space.
Original screen prompts and input guards remain authoritative.

OS key-repeat events are ignored. Held rotary keys repeat deliberately after
450 ms, then at 220 ms intervals when the input queue is available. Holding Enter
does not generate repeated movement presses; the server runs the original
long-press handler. Input is ignored while typing in form fields or using controls.
Blur/hidden events release all held inputs. The server also releases held pins
after 1.5 seconds without an input message and immediately on disconnect.

Zoom supports exact CSS factors 1–5 and integer Fit to Window. The canvas remains
240×240; zoom changes neither coordinates nor gameplay state. Fit scrolls rather
than shrinking fractionally below 1×. Default prefers 3× when it fits. Use 100%
browser zoom and integer OS display scaling for physical device-pixel comparisons.

## Manual playtest

The reproducible native acceptance route uses seed 2, a Fighter with Blades. Human
timing, movement, visibility and dice consumption can change the encounter’s course;
defeat remains a genuine possible outcome. No stats or monster outcomes are fixed
by the browser.

1. Confirm the original title, sprites and A: New Game / B: Load Game prompts.
2. Press Space for New Game. Use Up/Down to inspect the existing classes, then
   choose Fighter with Enter. Choose Blades with Enter.
3. Inspect the rolled character sheet; rotate to scroll it. Press Space to open
   the original acceptance menu and Space again to accept. Pause briefly between
   selections to respect the handheld’s transition guard.
4. In town, hold Enter for about a second to open the character sheet. Release
   Enter; Escape closes the sheet. Confirm blur or focus loss does not leave a
   button held.
5. Select Explore Dungeon in the original town list: from Stay Home, Down twice
   selects it. Press Space. Confirm the actual room tiles, Fighter sprite, facing
   cursor, door, object graphics and bottom message bar.
6. Press Enter once to move east one tile. Rotate to a wall and try stepping into
   it; confirm movement is blocked. Space opens the original map menu. Down/Up
   navigates it and Escape cancels. Restore east facing before continuing.
7. In the entrance room, walk east to and through its door. The native route takes
   12 eastward steps from the original (2,6) spawn. Confirm a real room transition,
   different geometry and generated creatures. Do not use coordinates as wire
   commands: only the virtual handheld inputs exist.
8. Let creatures notice/approach you, or explore toward them. Observe Roll for
   Initiative and turn messages. On your turn, Space opens Menu; select Attack.
   Use Ranged Attack when separated, Melee Attack when adjacent. Rotate to select
   a creature/direction and Space to confirm. Escape cancels targeting. Outside
   menus, Escape ends your combat turn where the original game permits it.
9. Observe attack rolls, HP changes, messages and enemy turns. Reopen the sheet to
   inspect current character state. Continue with supported attacks/actions until
   combat ends; confirm exploration and movement continue afterward. If the
   character dies, observe the actual death behavior; restart the foreground
   server for a fresh playtest if needed.
10. Exercise 1×–5× and Fit while in a static menu. Confirm crisp pixels and the
    same game state after zoom. Large full-screen transitions can take several
    chunks; normal cursor and tile updates are smaller.
11. Click Disconnect safely. Confirm the game pauses. Click Reconnect in that same
    tab and verify the same character, room and view. A second authenticated
    participant must receive RuntimeOccupied rather than your view.
12. Disconnect, then Ctrl+C in the NUC server terminal and Ctrl+C in the tunnel
    terminal. The server should print `stopped`. No daemon or public listener is
    created. All server state ends with the process.

Town Save/Load retains the original serialization code backed by process-memory
Preferences. It survives only within that server process. It is not an ESP32 save
import/export mechanism, and no persistence across server restarts is claimed.

## Build from the current source

```sh
cmake -S . -B build/playable -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DPFME_BUILD_SERVER=ON -DPFME_BUILD_PLAYABLE=ON -DPFME_BUILD_BROWSER_CAPTURE=ON
cmake --build build/playable --parallel 2
ctest --test-dir build/playable --output-on-failure
python3 tools/run_private_game.py --server build/playable/apps/server/pfme_server --seed 2
```

The server reuses the pinned Boost and Monocypher dependencies documented in
WEBSOCKET_PROTOCOL.md. The native runtime also uses the byte-identical Adafruit GFX
1.12.6 sources/font already vendored for the title capture, retaining its license.
No system package installation is required beyond the already installed compiler,
CMake and Python. On Windows use a configured CMake compiler environment and the
`.exe` filename. Keep the compiler runtime directory on PATH when using MinGW.

Both playable and server options default OFF. Original leaf/headless targets and
PlatformIO remain independent. The separate `apps/browser` offline title viewer
is preserved as a reference artifact; it is not the playable client.

