## Authority and multiplayer limit

The handheld uses global `player`, `dungeon`, `combat`, `tft`, menu state and many
file-static UI/targeting variables. They now execute on the NUC, not in each
browser. A Service-owned lease binds that one global runtime to a valid
GameSession participant and active authenticated connection. Another participant
cannot receive the controller’s pixels or change its state. Reconnect requires
the existing random ownership proof. Explicit leave retires this runtime until
process restart, preventing a recycled player ID from inheriting its private
state. Disconnect pauses ticks and preserves it.

Independent per-player locations/views, shared multi-room initiative and multiple
concurrent playable participants remain unimplemented. The exact next boundary is
to move authoritative world state out of globals and isolate player menu,
visibility, targeting and input state. Do not create independent worlds per browser
or broadcast the controller’s screen to a party. Existing ESP-NOW authority code is
compiled and remains available to the ESP32 firmware, but native radio transport
is unavailable and is not used as a substitute WebSocket multiplayer engine.

## Screen and input protocol extension

The existing authenticated protocol 1 adds `play`, `input` and `screen`; existing
limits remain 4 KiB requests, 16 KiB replies, 20 commands/sec and 8 connections.
Before a playable lease, the original topology API retains its previous behavior.

- `play` has no extra fields and requires an owned participant. It claims/resumes
  the single runtime, clears held pins and restarts the pixel stream at base 0.
- `input` accepts only `action` (`cw`, `ccw`, `encoder`, `a`, `b`, `release`) and
  Boolean `down`. No client positions, HP, inventory, clocks or dice are accepted.
- `screen` accepts decimal-string `since` and integer `offset`. It returns
  `encoding: rgb565-patch-rle1`, base/frame revisions as decimal strings, offset,
  total byte count, base64 `pixels`, and `more`. One immutable frame is pending;
  chunks contain at most 9,216 raw bytes. Invalid cursor/order is rejected.

Patch records are little-endian `start:uint16`, `encodedCount:uint16`, payload.
Count is the low 15 bits (1–32767). With bit 15 set, payload is one repeated
RGB565 color; otherwise payload is that many literal RGB565 colors. Start/count
must remain inside 57,600 pixels. Unchanged pixels are omitted. Records can cross
chunk boundaries, so the browser accumulates at most 230,400 bytes, validates the
complete frame and presents it atomically. It never mixes revisions or renders a
partial record. A newly claimed stream starts against a black framebuffer.

The client keeps one outstanding RPC and at most eight queued input transitions;
overflow collapses to release-all. Requests are paced at 75 ms or slower, including
screen pulls and controls, within existing limits. There is no unbounded queue or
idle stream of full frames. Frame IDs, bounded offsets and backpressure keep
production rendering independent of client speed. Reconnect fully resynchronizes
pixels. Zoom modifies only CSS dimensions and does not restart a session.

## Security, memory and known differences

Binding remains hard-coded to 127.0.0.1. The playable build serves only `/play`,
`/play.js`, `/play.css` from compiled fixed assets, with no filesystem routing or
URL parameters. HTTP assets contain no player state or secrets. Gameplay still
requires the gate, owned participant and lease. WebSocket Origin is either absent
(native client) or exactly `http://` plus the validated loopback Host/listener port;
null, foreign, alternate-host and multiple Origins are rejected. CSP disables
framing, external scripts and arbitrary connections. Auth/write/IO deadlines,
connection/rate/message limits and ownership proof handling remain enforced.

One native RGB565 surface uses 115,200 bytes. The Service screen stream holds two
more such pixel arrays and one bounded patch (at most 230,400 bytes). The browser
keeps one 230,400-byte RGBA accumulator, its canvas and bounded chunk buffers, plus
one transient 230,400-byte immutable presentation copy per changed frame and
runtime/compositor overhead. A CPU-friendly context and fresh presentation buffer
avoid stale pixelated compositor tiles during resynchronization. No new framebuffer
or adapter code enters ESP32 RAM.
Uncompressed full frames would cost 115,200 bytes before framing, or 153,600
base64 characters. Patch/RLE avoids sending repeated menu backgrounds and idle
pixels; actual traffic depends on changed sprites and screen transitions. All
gameplay and rendering remain on one serialized server event loop.

Audio output is silent. Native `delay()` advances the simulated clock without
blocking the entire service, so intermediate synchronous hit-flash frames can be
coalesced before the browser receives a snapshot. The original render calls still
run; exact flash/tearing/SPI timing and panel gamma are not claimed. A native
unavailable ESP-NOW notice may appear on the title message line. Native RNG is
reproducible across Windows/Ubuntu; matching an ESP32 random seed byte-for-byte is
not promised. No physical-panel visual comparison or ESP32 hardware execution was
performed in this phase.

No router, firewall, public DNS/proxy, container or persistent service configuration
is part of this build. No public exposure, Discord integration or Phase 3H work is
authorized by these instructions.

